Bringing writing you did not create in Skribisto into a project, for the first time, uses the
*Import documents* wizard. If instead you are bringing back a file you sent out of Skribisto for
someone else to mark up, that is a different question with a more exact answer: see
[the round trip](:help-round-trip).

## Starting it

Open *Work ▸ Import from ▸ Documents (Markdown, Word, ODT)…*. The wizard reads four formats:
Markdown (`.md`), Word (`.docx`), OpenDocument (`.odt`), and plain text (`.txt`). Drop one or more
files onto it, or use *Browse…* to pick them; reorder or remove any of them before moving on.
Nothing is written to your project until the last step.

## Four steps

*Files* collects what you are bringing in. *Review* is where the wizard shows you the tree it
would build, before any of it exists: each row's inferred type, the file it came from, its word
count, its scene breaks, and any comments it carries. Retype a row with its *Type* column, or
untick its *Import* checkbox to leave it (and everything under it) out. A *Heading levels* control
above the tree lets you change what a whole heading depth means at once, Heading 1 as Book,
Heading 2 as Chapter, and so on, rather than retyping row by row; if your files are a set of
chapters with no book heading at all, *Add top level* inserts one above everything the wizard
found.

*Destination* is where the new rows land. Choose a binder or an item in the live tree: the import
goes inside what you chose if it is a folder, or right after it if it is a scene. *Merge* only has
something to show when a row in the file matches what your project already holds; on a first
import into an empty spot it says so in one line and there is nothing to decide there. Finishing
runs the *Import* button itself: everything is written as one step, and a toast then says how many
items arrived, with an *Undo* action that stays up for a few seconds (the underlying undo entry
lasts far longer, reachable afterward through the ordinary undo history). If the write is refused
for any reason, the wizard stays open with your plan intact rather than reporting a finished import
that never happened.

## How the tree is built

The importer reads a file's headings and turns each one into a row. How deep a heading sits
decides whether it becomes a Book, Part, Chapter, or Scene, stepping down that ladder and stopping
at Scene: nothing sits inside a scene, so nothing deeper than the ladder's floor invents a phantom
level. See [the writing model](:help-writing-model) for what each of those is. A row can also be
retyped to Note or Paratext, off that structural ladder but still an available choice for any
heading.

A file with no headings at all becomes a single row holding all of its text. This is not treated
as a mistake, only noted for your information: it is exactly what a folder of one-scene-per-file
manuscripts looks like. If one file cannot be read, or has an extension nothing recognizes, only
that file is skipped and reported; the rest of the batch still comes in.

## Comments

A Word or OpenDocument file's comments come in too. Each one becomes a real Skribisto comment,
anchored to the exact words it was attached to, carrying its author and (from a `.docx`) their
initials, along with any replies underneath it. See [comments](:help-comments) for how they behave
once they are part of your project.
